unshar for OS-9/68000 -- what was changed, and why====================================================cshar 2.0 patchlevel 3, by Rich Salz, comp.sources.unix volume 15 (1988).ORIG/ holds every file of the three parts with the three official patchesapplied, as the patches leave them; ORIG/patches/ holds the patch postings.Only unshar is built here.  Built for the OS-9 freeware collection,2026-09-11.OS-9 has no /bin/sh to pipe a shell archive into, so unshar is built withUSE_MY_SHELL: it unpacks archives with its own small interpreter, parser.c,which knows the handful of commands shell archives use -- cat and sed withhere-documents, test, if, case, for, echo, mkdir, cd, and variables.Configuration (config.h)------------------------USE_MY_SHELL on; no popen, getpwuid or sys_errlist; index/rindex (OS-9's Clibrary has those, not strchr); the terminal is /term; off_t comes fromSRC/COMPAT's types.h and time_t is typedef'd here.For OS-9 (each marked OSK in the source)----------------------------------------lfiles.c   OS-9 has no stat().  A directory opens in directory mode,           _gs_size() gives a file's length, and a file that may be run           opens in execute mode.  Nothing is cached: the parser asks about           a file before writing it and again after.llib.c     mkdir() through mknod(); isatty() as "an SCF path"; Pid() for the           parser's $$, which lives in lexec.c beside fork() otherwise.lcwd.c     Cwd() names the data directory the way pd does, climbing `..'.glue.c     The temporary copy of the archive goes in the data directory --           OS-9 has no /tmp of its own -- through a buffer, since mktemp()           writes into its argument.unshar.c   stdout is left buffered.  After setbuf(stdout, NULL) this C           library writes each piece of printf's output as its LENGTH,           repeated: printf("sb3 unbuffered printf %s\n", "ok") wrote 22           bytes of 0x16, two of 0x02 and one 0x01.  putc() on the same           stream is correct, and so is fprintf() to stderr, which is           unbuffered from the start.  Measured under os9exec with a           five-line program built -qm; whether the library or the emulator           is at fault is not settled.Bugs in the posting, fixed--------------------------glue.c     With USE_MY_SHELL it could not have compiled: it opened TEMP,           which nothing defines, where it meant File.  And it closed the           archive -- which its caller closes again -- instead of the           temporary file it had just written, so that file was still in           its buffer when the parser reopened it and met end-of-file at           once.parser.c   test -f file -a "$1" != "-c" -- the clobber check most archives           make before each file -- answered true whenever the file was           ABSENT, so every file was refused as though it were already           there.Added to the interpreter, for archives made by other shar programs------------------------------------------------------------------parser.c   Quoted strings and letters run together are one word, as in sh:           '+ END-OF-FILE ''which6.c' is "+ END-OF-FILE which6.c".           "`wc -c < 'file'`" in double quotes -- how shar 3 writes its size           check -- is taken as the size test it is.           set `wc -c file` leaves the size in $1.           case accepts a branch on one line, "5970)  :;;", and a last           branch that runs to esac with no ;;.  Before, such an archive           was read past its esac into the files that followed.Measured--------Four real postings, unpacked on an RBF image and every file compared bymd5 with the same posting unpacked by sh on a Unix host (CR line endings,as OS-9 text has): comp.sources.misc v26i096 asciixmas (1 file), mod.sources.games v01i003 jive-val (4), comp.sources.unix v15 cshar part 1 (22) andcomp.sources.misc v17i012 which6 (3).  30 of 30 identical.An archive must have OS-9 line endings (CR) to be read a line at a time.  Afile brought over with LF endings is one enormous line; autolf converts it.